6328125, 0.048187255859375, -0.034515380859375, 0.00579833984375, 0.00568389892578125, 0.0163116455078125, -0.03106689453125, -0.015838623046875, 0.0062408447265625, -0.0020751953125, 0.00982666015625, -0.0156097412109375, 0.011016845703125, 0.0701904296875, 0.03656005859375, -0.00838470458984375, 0.007701873779296875, -0.0232696533203125, -0.00286865234375, 0.039520263671875, -0.04083251953125, -0.0181732177734375, -0.045501708984375, -0.0328369140625, 0.01177215576171875, 0.001049041748046875, 0.006343841552734375, -0.026641845703125, 0.0208282470703125, 0.0110015869140625, 0.01174163818359375, 0.003997802734375, -0.00789642333984375, -0.01021575927734375, 0.0075225830078125, -0.0156707763671875, 0.016387939453125, -0.00714111328125, 0.02069091796875, 0.01195526123046875, 0.01345062255859375, -0.006744384765625, 0.023223876953125, 0.032867431640625, -0.004360198974609375, 0.041534423828125, -0.020660400390625, -0.00852203369140625, 0.0050811767578125, -0.0202484130859375, -0.01136016845703125, 0.01348876953125], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Categorising invoices into the correct General Ledger (GL) code is essential for accurate financial reporting and tax compliance. However, this task is rendered complex by the diversity in organizational accounting practices\u2014a phenomenon we describe as \u201cunique accounting contexts.\u201d In practice, these contexts include differing vendor-specific GL code assignments (e.g., stationery purchases might be coded as office expenses for one client but as administrative overhead for another), custom and nested GL hierarchies, as well as diverse invoice description conventions, including local jargon and acronyms.\n\nThis diversity extends to how companies choose to group expenses: some aggregate all employee expenses under a single, broad code, while others use fine-grained codes for each category. To accommodate these idiosyncrasies, we systematically collected metadata about each organization\u2019s chart of accounts and GL-coding policies and encoded these as business rules into our dataset.\n\n**Methods**\n\nWe assessed whether small in-house language models (SLMs)\u2014specifically, SBERT (Sentence-BERT, a sentence transformer) and DeBERTa (a classic small transformer-based language model)\u2014can compete with larger, cloud-based models in accuracy, data security, and interpretability for this task. SLMs are defined here as transformer language models that are small enough for private, on-premises deployment yet capable of fine-tuning on specific organizational data. Previous studies [1,2] have noted that general-purpose models struggle with domain-specific terminology, require external API calls (raising data privacy concerns), and offer limited interpretability.\n\nOur dataset comprised 10,000 labeled invoices from mid-sized companies in manufacturing, consulting, and logistics, covering 45 GL codes. Label frequencies ranged from 3,000 invoices for the most common codes to 30 for the rarest, with the top 10 codes comprising 60% of labels. Data also spanned over 300 vendors and displayed varied invoice formats. Annotation used a two-stage process: initial pre-labeling by accounting software, followed by manual review by professional accountants. Ambiguous cases were independently coded by two annotators, with Cohen\u2019s kappa averaging 0.83 over a 500-sample subset. Disagreements were resolved by adjudication, and invoices with incomplete vendor histories were flagged and resolved via consensus whenever possible.\n\nAll invoices were de-identified and split into structured fields (vendor name, date, line items, total, description) along with raw OCR text. For structured models, only consistently present fields were used. Missing or ambiguous structured fields were handled by: (a) imputing entirely missing fields as blank (\u201c[BLANK]\u201d token), (b) excluding fields if present in <5% of invoices or if extraction confidence was below 0.4, in which case they did not contribute to feature construction. Data preprocessing included lower-casing, tokenization, duplicate removal, and conversion of numerical fields to standardized string representations.\n\nWe held out 10% of data (1,000 invoices), selected by stratified sampling across GL codes and vendors to preserve class diversity. Hyperparameters\u2014optimized using 5-fold cross-validation\u2014included: learning rate (2e-5 for SBERT, 1e-5 for DeBERTa), AdamW optimizer with weight decay 0.01, batch size 32, maximum 10 epochs, and early stopping with patience of 3 epochs (monitoring validation loss). Internal train/validation splits (90/10) ensured experimental consistency. Complete model and training configurations are listed in Supplementary Methods Table 1.\n\nBaselines were: (a) a zero-shot LLM (OpenAI GPT-3 via API) prompted as \u201cAssign the following invoice text to the most appropriate GL code from this list: [GL codes]. Invoice: [invoice text].\u201d Multiple prompt variants were tested, with \u22640.02 F1 variation; the best was used for reporting. (b) a vendor-based rule assigning the most common GL code from historical data per vendor\u2014a widely used industry fallback in the absence of automation.\n\n**Results**\n\nOur primary results are summarized in Table 1 below. On the held-out test set, SBERT reached 0.96 accuracy and 0.93 average (micro) F1; DeBERTa achieved 0.93 accuracy and 0.89 F1. Zero-shot LLM performance was lower at 0.83 accuracy and 0.81 F1; the vendor-based baseline was 0.74 accuracy and 0.68 F1. Macro-F1 scores were 0.84 (SBERT), 0.81 (DeBERTa), 0.69 (LLM), and 0.49 (vendor). The lowest per-class F1 for SBERT (for the rarest code) was 0.37, while the most common codes had F1 >0.97 across models. A summary of per-class F1 for the 10 most/least frequent codes is provided in Table 2, with the full breakdown in Supplementary Table 2.\n\nTo establish the robustness of results, we computed 95% confidence intervals using 1,000 bootstrap resamples of the test set (stratified by GL code). For example, SBERT accuracy 95% CI: [0.957, 0.965]; DeBERTa: [0.922, 0.937]. All differences between SLMs and baselines were statistically significant (p <0.01).\n\nBoth SLMs generalized well to new clients, with SBERT reaching 0.90 F1 after training on ~100 client-specific invoices. Rare GL codes (<1% of samples) remained challenging, but SLMs still outperformed baselines by at least 0.15 F1 on these classes.\n\nTo mitigate class imbalance, we used stratified sampling of train/test splits and incorporated class weighting in loss functions. When we piloted oversampling for rare codes, F1 increases were \u22640.01, accompanied by more unstable validation performance, so we did not adopt it for final models.\n\n**Interpretability**\n\nInterpreting model reasoning is crucial for financial applications. We explored interpretability by visualizing attention maps and intermediate activations. For example, SBERT\u2019s attention reliably concentrated on line item descriptions and vendor names, supporting the qualitative finding that these fields most strongly drive GL code predictions. In contrast, invoices with atypical language or from unseen vendors triggered more diffuse attention distributions, often leading to misclassifications\u2014a pattern that led some reviewers to flag such cases for further human review. Figure 1 displays representative attention maps: in one high-trust case, attention was focused almost exclusively on key item descriptions, while in an ambiguous case (new vendor + vague description) the model split attention across several fields, reflecting its uncertainty.\n\nTo further analyze representation quality, we quantitatively evaluated sentence embedding structure. Embeddings were globally anisotropic (i.e., unevenly distributed in the space, cf. [3]), but locally isotropic for a given vendor\u2014meaning invoices from a single vendor clustered compactly. Mean silhouette score across classes was 0.52, with intra-cluster Euclidean distance of 0.39\u00b10.05 and inter-cluster distance of 1.12\u00b10.22 (normalized units). Tighter clusters for frequent codes corresponded to higher per-class F1; rare classes had more diffuse clusters and lower F1, clarifying the source of misclassifications. Principal component projections revealed vendor identity as a strong but imperfect clustering signal, especially for frequent codes.\n\n**Comparisons to Prior Work**\n\nOur SLMs consistently outperformed both general-purpose LLMs and vendor-baseline approaches. Compared to [1], which reported domain-tuned transformer models achieving up to 0.89 F1 on invoice coding, our SBERT model surpasses this with 0.93 F1 on a similarly-sized, more vendor-diverse dataset. Sun et al. [2] describe custom NLP pipelines with F1 between 0.82-0.91 for structured invoices; our SLMs match or exceed these performances, even when using unstructured OCR text, reinforcing the value added by in-house model fine-tuning with rigorous vendor and code encoding.\n\nNotably, and contrary to [4] (which claimed that explicit structuring of invoice fields\u2014such as extracting line items and metadata\u2014yields significant performance gains in transformer models), we observed no significant SLM improvement when using structured fields over raw OCR text (\u0394F1 <0.01). This suggests SLMs efficiently extract key signals in unstructured forms and supports a less resource-intensive pipeline for many settings.\n\n**Discussion and Limitations**\n\nThis work demonstrates that small, fine-tuned SLMs can deliver high-accuracy, interpretable, and secure solutions for invoice categorization\u2014outperforming both LLMs and rule-based industry baselines. The methodological innovations\u2014detailed field encoding, consensus annotation, and embedding analysis\u2014yielded insights into both performance and error sources. All key model results and error analyses are summarized in Tables 1 and 2 and Figure 1 for reviewer reference.\n\nNonetheless, there are important limitations. Our focus on three industry sectors and ~10,000 invoices constrains generalizability to wholly novel accounting regimes or highly atypical invoice formats. Future work could expand to retail, healthcare, or public sector accounts and synthetically augment training data to better capture such diversity. Label noise likely persists, despite annotation safeguards: ambiguous invoice intent, human error, or incomplete vendor data can introduce errors, partially mitigated by consensus coding and flagging outliers. Finally, strong class imbalance (some GL codes <1% of cases) limited sensitivity to rare codes; as future mitigation, targeted data augmentation or active learning could help bolster minority performance.\n\nOperationally, SLMs provide enhanced data privacy (all processing remains in-house) and superior interpretability (intermediate activations and attention maps easily visualized). However, these benefits entail ongoing maintenance: models require periodic retraining to accommodate evolving accounting rules, new vendor relationships, and shifts in"}}